//! Shared-memory ring transport, spec v2: rings live in client memory.
//!
//! Each client allocates a ring block inside its own shared memory (memory32
//! or memory64) and hands moonbeam the memory plus the block's byte offset.
//! Moonbeam never exposes its own memory. All access to the client memory
//! goes through a small per-client bridge module (`shm_shim.wat`) that
//! imports both memories (multi-memory) and copies frames between them.
//! Moonbeam calls the bridge directly through its function table, so frames
//! never pass through JS.
//!
//! Every position is a byte offset relative to the block base. All fields
//! are little-endian u32, atomics are 4-byte aligned.
//!
//! ```text
//! block (64-byte aligned, written by the client before attaching)
//!   0  magic        "MBRB"
//!   4  version      2
//!   8  block_size   total bytes; both rings must fit inside
//!  12  slot_count   power of two >= 2
//!  16  slot_size    multiple of 4, >= max_frame + 4, <= 131072
//!  20  max_frame    60..=65535
//!  24  to_client    ring offset (64-aligned): moonbeam -> client
//!  28  to_host      ring offset (64-aligned): client -> moonbeam
//!  32  state        see STATE_*
//!
//! ring (128-byte header, then slot_count slots)
//!   0  head         producer index, free-running u32
//!   4  seq          futex word, bumped by the producer after each publish
//!   8  waiting      consumer sets 1 before sleeping on seq
//!  12  dropped      frames the producer dropped because the ring was full
//!  64  tail         consumer index, free-running u32
//! 128  slots        slot n at 128 + (n & (slot_count - 1)) * slot_size
//!
//! slot: u32 len, then len bytes of frame
//! ```
//!
//! Producer: full if `head - tail == slot_count`; otherwise write the slot,
//! store `head + 1`, bump `seq`, and if `waiting.swap(0) != 0` notify `seq`.
//! Consumer: empty if `tail == head`; otherwise copy the slot out and store
//! `tail + 1`. To sleep: load `seq`, store `waiting = 1`, check the ring
//! again, and only `Atomics.wait(seq, loaded)` if it is still empty.
//!
//! Lifecycle: the client writes the block and sets state READY. The bridge's
//! `init` validates it and moves it to ATTACHED. The client asks to leave by
//! setting CLOSING; moonbeam stops using the block and sets RELEASED, after
//! which the client may reuse the memory. Moonbeam can also release on its own.
//!
//! Fairness: each `poll` lets a client consume at most `max_slots_per_client`
//! slots, counting slots the bridge skipped for a bad length, so a client
//! that floods its ring with garbage still only gets its share of the thread.
//!
//! `js/client.js` is a reference client; `js/moonbeam.js` drives this from JS.